<p>Residential property claims in Corpus Christi are often more complicated than they look from the street. A single storm can damage a roof, push rain through window frames, and send surge water into the garage, and each of those may fall under a different policy. Corpus Christi Public Adjuster represents homeowners, not insurers. We help you sort out which coverage applies, document the full loss, and present a claim that reflects what it actually costs to repair your home.</p>

<h2>The Homes We Help With</h2>
<p>We work with owners of single-family homes, townhomes, condos (for the owner's portion of coverage), duplexes, and small rental properties across Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and surrounding Coastal Bend communities such as <a href="/service-areas/portland-tx/">Portland</a>, Rockport, and Padre Island. Local housing has a few features that shape how claims play out:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Slab-on-grade construction.</strong> Water that enters at floor level wicks into drywall, baseboards, and cabinets quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Composition shingle and metal roofs.</strong> Both can show hail and wind damage that is easy to miss from the ground.</li>
<li><strong>Brick veneer, stucco, and fiber cement siding.</strong> Cracks and impact marks may need close inspection to separate new storm damage from older wear.</li>
<li><strong>Elevated homes on the islands.</strong> Damage below the living area, such as enclosures and stairs, often raises flood-versus-wind questions.</li>
<li><strong>Salt-air exposure.</strong> Insurers sometimes point to corrosion or age. Careful documentation helps separate long-term wear from sudden storm damage.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Homeowners, TWIA, and Flood: Which Policy Covers What</h2>
<p>Many coastal homeowners carry three policies. The table below is a general overview; your own policy forms and declarations page control.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Typically covers</th><th>Common issues</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Homeowners policy (often with a wind/hail exclusion)</td><td>Fire, smoke, theft, sudden plumbing leaks, and other covered perils</td><td>Exclusions for wear, mold limits, gradual leaks</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA windstorm policy</td><td>Wind and hail damage only, for eligible properties in the catastrophe area</td><td>Wind versus surge disputes, WPI-8 certificate questions, percentage deductibles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood policy (NFIP or private)</td><td>Rising water, storm surge, and overland flooding</td><td>Separate building and contents limits, 30-day waiting period on most new NFIP policies</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The <a href="https://www.twia.org/about-us/overview/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Windstorm Insurance Association</a> states that its policies cover wind and hail losses only. NFIP residential coverage is available up to $250,000 for the building and $100,000 for contents, according to <a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/whats-covered" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FloodSmart.gov</a>. For more on the windstorm side, see our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page.</p>

<h2>When More Than One Adjuster Is Involved</h2>
<p>After a hurricane or major storm, a homeowner may deal with a TWIA adjuster, a flood adjuster, and a homeowners adjuster, sometimes all in the same week. Each one looks at the loss through the lens of its own policy. That can leave gaps. The wind adjuster may attribute interior water damage to surge, while the flood adjuster attributes it to rain coming through a damaged roof. When each insurer points to the other, the homeowner can end up with neither paying for the same wall.</p>
<p>Clear evidence helps close those gaps: water lines on walls, photos of roof openings above wet ceilings, debris lines outside, and weather and tide records for the date of loss. We help organize that evidence so each insurer can see which part of the damage falls under its policy. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge damage</a> explains how these disputes typically arise. Keep separate files for each claim, and note each policy's deductible, because a windstorm deductible stated as a percentage of the dwelling limit can be much larger than the flat deductible on a homeowners policy.</p>

<h2>Common Residential Claim Types</h2>
<h3>Storm, wind, and hail</h3>
<p>Spring storms bring hail, which NOAA records show peaking in April and May in Nueces County, and strong thunderstorm winds. The May 8, 2025 windstorm produced NWS-estimated gusts of 105 to 115 mph around Petronila, Driscoll, and Agua Dulce. See <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Hurricanes and tropical storms</h3>
<p>Harvey in 2017 and Hanna in 2020 are recent reminders that the Coastal Bend is exposed to wind, wind-driven rain, and surge. Hurricane claims often involve named-storm or windstorm deductibles and more than one insurer. Our <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> page covers those issues.</p>
<h3>Water and freeze damage</h3>
<p>Sudden plumbing leaks, water heater failures, and freeze-related pipe breaks like those during the February 2021 winter storm can cause extensive interior damage. Coverage often turns on whether the water was sudden or gradual.</p>
<h3>Fire and smoke</h3>
<p>Kitchen, electrical, and garage fires frequently cause smoke and soot damage well beyond the burned area, plus water damage from firefighting.</p>

